[
  "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The scene is captured from a 3/4 overhead perspective at 40 degrees, ensuring the texture and contents of two rustic ceramic bowls are fully visible. The primary bowl is sharp in the foreground, with the second bowl slightly out of focus behind it. The depth of field is shallow, creating a soft, natural bokeh background. Lighting is soft and directional, streaming from a window on the left, casting gentle shadows and creating a subtle sheen on the broth surface. The setting is a rustic kitchen featuring an aged oak wood surface with visible grain. Props include a weathered wooden spoon resting casually against the bowl and a few scattered, bright green cilantro leaves and a small stray yellow corn kernel on the wood grain. The chili displays a rich, savory aesthetic; the lean ground turkey is broken into small, tender morsels and is mixed with plump, pearly white cannellini beans and vibrant, bright yellow sweet corn. Small flecks of green chiles and freshly chopped, verdant cilantro are distributed throughout the warm, amber-colored broth, which has a natural, slightly thick consistency. One bowl shows a tiny, realistic drip of broth on the outer matte ceramic rim. The overall color palette is warm and inviting, dominated by earthy browns, golden yellows, and fresh green accents. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. The scene should look like someone just finished cooking and plating — lived-in, warm, authentic. Not a sterile studio setup. Slight natural imperfections: asymmetric garnish placement, an uneven distribution of ingredients, a slight oil sheen on the surface. No plastic sheen, no unnaturally smooth surfaces, no perfect symmetry. Warm, golden-hour color temperature. No text, no watermark, no logo, no hands, no faces. The image should be indistinguishable from a photograph taken by a professional food photographer for a printed cookbook."
]
